Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[Cell[BoxData[
   FormBox["\<\"The **Viterbi algorithm** is a dynamic programming algorithm \
used to find the most likely sequence of hidden states in a hidden Markov \
model (HMM). It is widely applied in various fields such as signal \
processing, speech recognition, and bioinformatics. In the context of this \
paper, the Viterbi algorithm is employed to search for gravitational wave \
(GW) signals from primordial black hole (PBH) binaries in a model-agnostic \
way.\\n\\n### Key Concepts:\\n- **Hidden Markov Model (HMM)**: A statistical \
model where the system being modeled is assumed to follow a Markov process \
with hidden states. The observable data is generated by these hidden states, \
but the states themselves are not directly observable.\\n- **Dynamic \
Programming**: A method for solving complex problems by breaking them down \
into simpler subproblems. The Viterbi algorithm uses this approach to \
efficiently compute the most probable sequence of hidden states.\\n\\n### \
Application in the Paper:\\nIn this study, the Viterbi algorithm is used to \
track the evolution of the frequency of gravitational wave signals over time. \
Unlike other methods (e.g., Frequency-Hough), which assume a specific \
power-law behavior for the signal, the Viterbi algorithm is \
**model-agnostic**. This means it can detect arbitrary patterns in the \
time-frequency domain, making it particularly useful for exploring the \
intermediate mass range of PBHs where higher-order post-Newtonian (PN) \
effects may become significant.\\n\\nThe algorithm works by:\\n1. **Defining \
a set of possible states** (e.g., different frequency bins).\\n2. **Assigning \
probabilities** to transitions between states based on the observed data \
(e.g., the GW signal).\\n3. **Finding the most likely sequence** of states \
(i.e., the most probable frequency evolution of the signal) using dynamic \
programming.\\n\\nIn this paper, the Viterbi algorithm is favored for its \
computational efficiency and flexibility, even though it may be less \
sensitive than the Frequency-Hough method.\"\>", TextForm]], "InlineText",
   ExpressionUUID->"03785f9b-81b6-4ae8-9fe9-17176407657d"],
  Text["The **Viterbi algorithm** is a dynamic programming algorithm used to \
find the most likely sequence of hidden states in a hidden Markov model \
(HMM). It is widely applied in various fields such as signal processing, \
speech recognition, and bioinformatics. In the context of this paper, the \
Viterbi algorithm is employed to search for gravitational wave (GW) signals \
from primordial black hole (PBH) binaries in a model-agnostic way.\n\n### Key \
Concepts:\n- **Hidden Markov Model (HMM)**: A statistical model where the \
system being modeled is assumed to follow a Markov process with hidden \
states. The observable data is generated by these hidden states, but the \
states themselves are not directly observable.\n- **Dynamic Programming**: A \
method for solving complex problems by breaking them down into simpler \
subproblems. The Viterbi algorithm uses this approach to efficiently compute \
the most probable sequence of hidden states.\n\n### Application in the Paper:\
\nIn this study, the Viterbi algorithm is used to track the evolution of the \
frequency of gravitational wave signals over time. Unlike other methods \
(e.g., Frequency-Hough), which assume a specific power-law behavior for the \
signal, the Viterbi algorithm is **model-agnostic**. This means it can detect \
arbitrary patterns in the time-frequency domain, making it particularly \
useful for exploring the intermediate mass range of PBHs where higher-order \
post-Newtonian (PN) effects may become significant.\n\nThe algorithm works \
by:\n1. **Defining a set of possible states** (e.g., different frequency \
bins).\n2. **Assigning probabilities** to transitions between states based on \
the observed data (e.g., the GW signal).\n3. **Finding the most likely \
sequence** of states (i.e., the most probable frequency evolution of the \
signal) using dynamic programming.\n\nIn this paper, the Viterbi algorithm is \
favored for its computational efficiency and flexibility, even though it may \
be less sensitive than the Frequency-Hough method."]]], "Output",
 TaggingRules->{},
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9365171611406403`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[430]=",
 CellID->489968845,ExpressionUUID->"0ca68bd2-6b2e-4e68-aed8-d468a7ea10f3"]
}, Open  ]],

